Let me analyze the bug in this debounce.ts module.

The debounce function:

```typescript
export function debounce(fn: (...args: any[]) => void, waitMs: number) {
    let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
    return (...args: any[]) => {
        if (timer !== null) {
            return; // already scheduled
        }
        timer = setTimeout(() => fn(args), waitMs);
        timer = null;
    };
}
```

The bug: The function resets `timer = null` immediately after scheduling, but doesn't clear the pending `setTimeout`. So when a new call comes in while the timer is pending, the `if (timer !== null)` check passes (timer is null because it was reset), so it schedules a new timer without cancelling the previous one. This results in the function being called multiple times (once for each call), instead of once after waitMs from the last call.

The correct debounce implementation:

```typescript
export function debounce(fn: (...args: any[]) => void, waitMs: number) {
    let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | null = null;
    return (...args: any[]) => {
        if (timer !== null) {
            clearTimeout(timer);
            return;
        }
        timer = setTimeout(() => {
            timer = null;
            fn(...args);
        }, waitMs);
    };
}
```

This:
1. If there's already a pending timer, clear it and return (cancelling the pending call).
2. Otherwise, schedule the timer. When it fires, set timer to null and call fn with the latest args.

Note: `fn(...args)` — spreading args. Actually, `fn(args)` works too since args is an array. But `fn(...args)` is the common pattern. Either works since args is an array. Let me keep it consistent.

